<!--
    The small icon Android puts in the status bar and beside every notification.

    ## Why it is a silhouette and not the app icon

    Android draws this by taking the alpha channel and painting it white, on
    every version since Lollipop. Hand it a colour bitmap - which the launcher
    icon is - and every pixel with any opacity becomes solid white, so a rounded
    square logo arrives as a plain white square. That is what "the notification
    icon is a grey blob" always is.

    So this is drawn for the job: one shape, white where the mark is,
    transparent everywhere else, at the 24dp Android expects.

    The mark is PINGO's P with its dot. Kept geometric rather than traced from
    the logo, because at status-bar size - often 18 physical pixels - fine
    curves close up into mush, and a shape that survives being small is worth
    more than one that matches at 512.
-->
<vector xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:width="24dp"
    android:height="24dp"
    android:viewportWidth="24"
    android:viewportHeight="24"
    android:tint="#FFFFFF">

    <!-- The stem. -->
    <path
        android:fillColor="#FFFFFF"
        android:pathData="M6,3 h3 v18 h-3 z" />

    <!--
        The bowl, as a filled ring section rather than a stroked curve. A stroke
        thins as the icon scales down; a filled shape keeps its weight.
    -->
    <path
        android:fillColor="#FFFFFF"
        android:pathData="M9,3 h4.2 a5.4,5.4 0 0 1 0,10.8 h-4.2 v-3 h4.2 a2.4,2.4 0 0 0 0,-4.8 h-4.2 z" />

    <!-- The dot. It is the whole personality of the mark; it stays. -->
    <path
        android:fillColor="#FFFFFF"
        android:pathData="M18.4,3.2 m-2.1,0 a2.1,2.1 0 1,0 4.2,0 a2.1,2.1 0 1,0 -4.2,0" />
</vector>
